<h1>Unit 13: Ideal Gas</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Heat &amp; Thermodynamics</p><p class="summary">The ideal gas is a model with a stated domain: it works when molecules are far apart and barely interacting. Its real value is that it links temperature to microscopic motion, so the same equation can be read as a statement about atoms.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>PV = nRT with R = 8.314462618 J/(mol.K) exactly in the 2019 SI, where n is the amount of substance in moles.</li><li>Boyle's, Charles's and Avogadro's laws are limiting cases of the ideal-gas equation, valid at low density and at temperatures well above condensation.</li><li>Kinetic theory gives P = (1/3)(N/V) m &lt;v^2&gt;, so the pressure of a gas is the momentum transfer from its molecules hitting the walls.</li><li>For a monatomic gas the mean translational kinetic energy is (3/2) k T, and the rms speed is sqrt(3RT/M).</li><li>The internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature, U = n C_V T, and for a monatomic gas C_V = 3R/2.</li><li>Mayer's relation C_P - C_V = R follows from the first law together with the ideal-gas equation.</li><li>The ideal gas law is a limit rather than a definition: it holds exactly only at infinite dilution, and real gases show the condensation and critical behaviour the law cannot describe.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The model needs negligible molecular volume and negligible intermolecular forces: low density and a temperature well above the condensation temperature.</li><li>The van der Waals equation (P + a n^2/V^2)(V - nb) = nRT corrects the two main failures - attractive forces (a) and finite molecular size (b).</li><li>Equipartition, and therefore the plateau value of the heat capacity, fails at low temperature where the quantum energy levels are not thermally populated.</li><li>A gas in equilibrium has a single pressure and temperature; a non-equilibrium gas has neither, so the equation of state cannot even be written down.</li><li>Kinetic theory needs equipartition, which fails once the quantum level spacing is comparable to kT - exactly why helium must be cooled below about 4 K to liquefy.</li></ul><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Molar gas constant (exact)</td><td>8.314462618 J/(mol.K)</td></tr><tr><td>Room-temperature rms speed of N2</td><td>about 517 m/s</td></tr><tr><td>Standard molar volume at 0 degrees C, 1 atm</td><td>22.414 L/mol</td></tr><tr><td>Boiling point of liquid nitrogen at 1 atm</td><td>about 77 K (-196 degrees C)</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can translational motion be cooled below the quantum ground state of the confining potential?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Laser and evaporative cooling reach picokelvin temperatures for motion, but a confinement potential has a lowest level that cannot be crossed; how that floor depends on the geometry is still open.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Why does the heat capacity of a gas rise in steps at low temperature while a solid's varies smoothly?</b></p><p class="status">Under active scrutiny</p><p>Rotational and vibrational levels freeze out one by one below their characteristic temperatures - the Debye, rotor and oscillator models - which is settled for idealised cases and still refined for real materials.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Compute the rms speed of nitrogen molecules at room temperature and compare it with the speed of sound in air.</li><li>Use the first law and C_V to show that the internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature.</li><li>Plot the P-V diagrams of an ideal gas and a van der Waals gas and mark the region where real behaviour departs.</li></ol><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/13-3-the-ideal-gas-law">OpenStax: the ideal gas law</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-1-molecular-model-of-an-ideal-gas">OpenStax: Molecular Model of an Ideal Gas</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/2-3-heat-capacity-and-equipartition-of-energy">OpenStax: Heat Capacity and Equipartition of Energy</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
